## Context

Release audit for v0.2.0 (security seat, 2026-10-29) on main d8f4f6f. Dropping `requests` (0021) lost
its redirect hygiene. `sources/_http.py:71-73` only caps the redirect count; urllib copies every header
to the redirect target, so the ISTEX `Authorization: Bearer` token (`sources/istex.py:112`) follows a
cross-host redirect. Reproduced: `HTTPRedirectHandler().redirect_request(Request("https://a/x",
headers={"Authorization": "Bearer S"}), None, 302, "", {}, "https://evil.example/y").headers` keeps it.
Also: the opener keeps urllib's file/ftp handlers (`Session().get("file:///etc/hostname")` reads the
file; reachable through the library's `Request(kind="url")`), redirects may leave HTTPS, and
`resp.read()` plus gzip/zlib decompression are unbounded (`_http.py:76-85,160`).

## Actions
1. Drop `Authorization` (and `Cookie` set by hand, if any) when a redirect changes host; refuse an
   https -> http downgrade.
2. Accept only `http` and `https` URLs, initial and redirected.
3. Cap the response body and the decompressed size (a constant generous for PDFs, e.g. 200 MB); over
   the cap -> `failed` with a reason, no partial file.
4. Tests: each of the above, red first.

Pre-release fix: lands before the v0.2.0 tag.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] A cross-host redirect does not carry the Authorization header (test)
- [ ] `file://`, `ftp://` and an https->http redirect are refused (tests)
- [ ] Oversized and decompression-bomb bodies fail cleanly without a partial file (tests)
- [ ] Suite and gate pass
